\par }\pard {\f5 The Dc Bias that can be applied to the }{\f5\uldb RX Antenna}{\v\f5 RX_Antenna}{\f5 
 can be used to power wide band active  antennas. The circuit diagram shown is an example of this type of antenna. This antenna covers the complete spectrum, 450 KHz to 30 MHz, of the DSP100. It has about the same performance as a dipole over the complete
 range. An active antenna of this type is basically an impedance converter transforming the 50 ohm input of the DSP100 to about 100 kilo ohms 
shunted by about 6 pico farads.  At 30 MHz the antenna looks like a short antenna with a very low noise preamplifier. As the frequency is lowered the loss of the antenna increases at about the same rate as the noise in the atmosphere increases, giving a c
onstant signal to noise ratio over the entire HF spectrum. These antennas cannot perform as well as beam antennas as the beam antenna is limiting the noise in unwanted directions compared to the signal.
